-> Practical :
Connect to a VM via ssh connection on mac terminal , install terraform for Amazon Linux .

sudo mkdir 01-tf-script
cd 01-tf-script
sudo touch main.tf dev.tfvars prod.tfvars provider.tf qa.tfvars variables.tf

› Configure these files as

sudo vi provider.tf
i
provider "aws" {
region     = "ap-south-1"
access_key = " "
secret_key = " "
}
esc      +       : wq

sudo vi main.tf
i
resource "aws_instance" "my_vm" {
ami           = var.ami_id
instance_type = var.instance_type
tags = {
Name = "Server-${terraform.workspace}"
}
}
esc      +       : wq
sudo vi dev.tfvars
i
ami_id        = "ami-00bb6a80f01f03502"
instance_type = "t2.micro"
esc      +       : wq

sudo vi prod.tfvars
i
ami_id        = "ami-00bb6a80f01f03502"
instance_type = "t2.medium"
esc      +       : wq
sudo vi qa.tfvars
i
ami_id        = "ami-00bb6a80f01f03502"
instance_type = "t2.small"
esc      +       : wq

sudo vi variables.tf
i
variable "ami_id" {
type  =  string
}
variable "instance_type" {
type  =  string
}
esc      +       : wq

sudo terraform init
sudo terraform fmt
sudo terraform validate
sudo terraform workspace list
default

sudo terraform workspace new dev
Created and switched to workspace "dev"!

*You're now on a new, empty workspace. Workspaces isolate their state,so if you run "terraform plan" Terraform will not see any existing resources in this configuration.

sudo terraform workspace list
default
dev

sudo terraform plan --var-file=dev.tfvars
sudo terraform apply --var-file=dev.tfvars --auto-approve
Now check AWS console , dev server will be created (Server-dev)
sudo terraform workspace new prod
Created and switched to workspace "prod"!
sudo terraform workspace list
default
dev
prod
sudo terraform plan --var-file=prod.tfvars
sudo terraform apply --var-file=prod.tfvars --auto-approve

› Now check AWS console , prod server will be created (Server-prod)
sudo terraform workspace new qa
Created and switched to workspace "qa"!

› sudo terraform workspace list
default
dev
prod
qa
sudo terraform plan --var-file=qa.tfvars
sudo terraform apply --var-file=qa.tfvars --auto-approve

› Now check AWS console , qa server will be created (Server-qa)
› Now total 3 instances are running on console .

› To check where workspaces are created inside our local machine folder hit command ~
ls -a
cd .terraform.tfstate.d
ls
dev   prod   qa
cd dev
ls
terraform.tfstate

› Now to destroy the environments -
sudo terraform destroy --var-file=qa.tfvars --auto-approve
sudo terraform workspace select prod
sudo terraform destroy --var-file=prod.tfvars --auto-approve
sudo terraform workspace select dev
sudo terraform destroy --var-file=dev.tfvars --auto-approve
